2211 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 861 0 R
/E (Films can raise awareness and attractiveness of tourism destinations but they have also a power to shape how places are seen. There are several humorous cult films in Estonia but the filming locations have not been used for their full potential in place-making. This paper focuses on Sense of the place theory and how it could explain why those locations of beloved humorous films have not become popular places to visit among tourists and why locals do not commodify the opportunities given by the storylines. The specific focus of the paper is: How do locals sense the place of famous film sets?)
/Pg 124 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
2212 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 861 0 R
/E (A qualitative research approach, using semi-structured interviews, was selected to capture rich, in-depth knowledge. Based on the data collected from 24 local residents living near film sets the results show that all of ten Sense of the place indicators \(Gokce & Chen, 2018\)  appear in current case, also being closely related to the reasons why locals do not wish to commodify their home, even if the films give them plenty of opportunities. Locals are proud that they live in Muhu, proud of their celebrities, and also happy that their home was selected for a movie, yet home is considered special. The main reason why those filming locations of Estonian old cult films are not active spots for visitors to see, lays in the nature of native locals, who prefer safety and privacy over economic income.)
/Pg 124 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
