2479 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 893 0 R
/T (Portuguese literary walks and routes: Practices and recommendations)
/E (Portuguese literary walks and routes: Practices and recommendations)
/Pg 156 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
2480 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 893 0 R
/E (The research pursues two main objectives. First, it undertakes a systematic mapping and review of literary walking projects across various regions of Portugal. This exploration is achieved through a comparative content analysis focusing on variables such as project authorship, funding sources, stated objectives, visitor information, destinations, points of interest, route length, level of difficulty, duration, terminology, technological integration, and the selection and incorporation of literary elements. Second, the study examines an in-person literary walking project in Lisbon \(www.passeiosliterarios.com\) through an analysis of semi-structured interviews with seven professional tourist guides. Tourist guides mediate between "the toured object" and visitors by "directing, commanding, narrating and re-enacting." \(Roesch, 2009, p. 185\). Accordingly, this study examines how guides design and deliver these literary tourism experiences centred on Portuguese literary heritage, with a particular focus on the interplay between urban landscapes and fictional references, audience characteristics, featured authors, and communication strategies. The research findings reveal that the terminology used to market literary walks and routes is heterogeneous, that the use of technology is limited, that women literary writers are seldom the theme of these tours, that Alentejo is the region with the smallest number of walking tours and that most projects provide little information about distance, difficulty and directions. Furthermore, the research also reveals that tour guides' challenges include balancing literary information and leisure to keep the group engaged, adapting to group dynamics, and ensuring the group's safety in Lisbon's urban settings.)
/Pg 156 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
